WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:04.940
Venom has arrived, and the public just can't
seem to get enough of the world's most popular

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.710
brain-eating alien symbiote.

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:10.660
But while you were distracted by that slippery,
slimy tongue, you might have missed some of

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:12.340
the film's more subtle moments.

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:15.180
Here's a look at some small details you missed
in Venom.

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:17.100
Source material

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:22.560
One thing is very clear in almost every scene
of Venom: the people who made the movie are

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.280
big fans of the comic book.

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:28.320
Some of it is pretty obvious, like the fact
that the Life Foundation was front and center

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:31.420
in a number of plotlines involving the alien
symbiotes.

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:32.640
But there's plenty beyond that.

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:37.140
For instance, there's the idea that the Life
Foundation's original goal was to cure cancer.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:41.760
That comes from the origin story of the Ultimate
Marvel Universe's version of Venom.

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:43.780
Then there are specific sequences.

00:00:43.790 --> 00:00:48.570
For instance, the scene where Riot rips Venom
out of Eddie's host body is an almost exact

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:52.120
re-creation of the cover of Venom: Carnage
Unleashed #3.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:56.330
And there are even specific lines of dialogue
borrowed from the comics, like the line

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:02.300
"Eyes, lungs, pancras, so many snacks, so
little time."

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:06.820
which originally appeared in the pages of
Amazing Spider-Man #374.

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:08.900
Creator callouts

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:14.100
Venom features all sorts of references to
the writers and artists who created the character,

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:19.120
most prominently when Eddie's ex-girlfriend
Anne mentions the name of her law firm: Michelinie

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:19.860
and McFarlane.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:24.980
Those names, of course, belong to writer David
Michelinie and artist Todd McFarlane, who

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:28.280
co-created Venom in Amazing Spider-Man #299.

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:30.400
But some references are more obscure.

00:01:30.409 --> 00:01:34.510
Near Eddie's Chinatown apartment, for instance,
a sign in the background in the final scene

00:01:34.510 --> 00:01:39.630
reads "Ron Lim's Herbals," which is a reference
to artist Ron Lim, who pencilled the Venom:

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:42.620
Lethal Protector mini-series that much of
the film is based on.

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:44.700
And the apartment building Eddie lives in?

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:49.720
That would be The Schueller Building, named
for Randy Schueller, a fan who sent his idea

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:54.900
for a black Spider-Man costume to Marvel in
the 1980s, inadvertently providing the original

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:56.860
inspiration for Venom in the first place.

00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:57.740
Nice!

00:01:58.620 --> 00:01:59.580
Deadly doggie

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:05.240
One of Venom's many funny moments is the all
too brief sequence in the hospital when, separated

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:10.149
from Eddie Brock, the Venom symbiote is forced
to use a tiny little doogie named Gemini as

00:02:10.149 --> 00:02:11.460
a host in order to survive.

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:16.279
That's actually a subtle reference to the
comics, where not one, but two dogs have been

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:18.320
turned into symbiotic monsters.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:23.000
One was Ivan the a sled dog, while the other
was a german shepard named Samson.

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:27.659
Both symbiote dogs proved that when it comes
to pet ownership, good breeding does indeed

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:28.160
matter.

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:32.400
So if you suspect your pet has bonded with
an alien killer, please consider getting it

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:33.460
spayed or neutered.

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:35.580
The symbiote Scream

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:40.400
At the beginning of the film, it's mentioned
that there are five alien lifeforms being

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:41.480
brought back to Earth.

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:42.520
One is Venom.

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:46.340
One is Riot, who bonds with the movie's villain,
Carlton Drake.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:51.340
One is apparently Agony, the blue-ish symbiote
who kills poor Dr. Dora Skirth before dying

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:51.840
itself.

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:57.220
And one is probably Carnage, who may or may
not be attached to serial killer Cletus Cassidy

00:02:57.220 --> 00:02:59.880
by the time the first post-credits scene hits
the screen.

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:04.600
But there's also a fifth symbiote who seemingly
vanishes partway through the film: the yellow

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:07.840
symbiote that briefly bonds with a super cute
little bunny rabbit.

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:12.860
Exactly which symbiote this is and what happened
to it are both mysteries, but while some speculate

00:03:12.870 --> 00:03:17.989
that the bunny might have housed the symbiote
known as Phage, the smart money is on another

00:03:17.989 --> 00:03:20.120
yellow-tinted symbiote: Scream.

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:24.150
In the comics, both Phage and Scream were
created by the Life Foundation, but Scream

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:28.529
eventually turned on the others and killed
all their host forms, believing symbiotes

00:03:28.529 --> 00:03:29.100
to be evil.

00:03:29.849 --> 00:03:33.879
Given that the film seems to be setting up
Venom vs. Carnage for the sequel, having a

00:03:33.879 --> 00:03:38.769
loose cannon like Scream at large and looking
to eliminate both of them could be a fun and

00:03:38.769 --> 00:03:39.800
interesting twist.

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:41.700
The Wilhelm Scream

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:47.060
It's not just comic book fans that got serviced
with in-jokes, movie buffs also got their

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:51.359
own callout thanks to the prominent use of
the legendary Wilhelm Scream during the scene

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:52.700
where Venom fights the SWAT team.

00:03:58.860 --> 00:03:59.680
"(Wilhelm Scream)"

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:02.520
The audio was actually first used way back

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:07.800
in 1951 for the film The Distant Drums during
a scene when a man was eaten by an alligator.

00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:11.620
"(Wilhelm Scream)"

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:17.340
Named for Private Wilhelm, a character who
gives this pained cry in 1953's The Charge

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:22.750
at Feather River after being shot by an arrow,
the sound effect was revived in 1977's Star

00:04:22.750 --> 00:04:26.060
Wars when a stormtrooper fell to his doom
inside the Death Star.

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:30.640
It subsequently became a tradition for Star
Wars and Indiana Jones films, and then spread

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:33.940
to everything from Reservoir Dogs to Beauty
and the Beast.

00:04:34.060 --> 00:04:36.800
Pretty exciting that Venom is keeping the
tradition alive.

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:40.320
"(Wilhelm Scream)"

